WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.290
Film Courage: Have you had a moment in your
life where you say “I’m going to make

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:04.930
movies for the rest of my life?”

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:10.400
Or you’ve never really had to have this
moment where there is light beaming down on

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.330
you and…you know what I’m saying?

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.059
Has it just been where you’ve gone from
one film to the next or has there been someone

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.732
where this voice has come to you and said
“I’ve got to do this for the rest of my

00:00:20.732 --> 00:00:21.732
life.

00:00:21.732 --> 00:00:22.732
You’ve were meant to do this.”

00:00:22.732 --> 00:00:23.732
Matthew Berkowitz, Filmmaker: No it’s every
day.

00:00:23.732 --> 00:00:28.310
I wake up every day and I have the voice telling
me “You have to do this for the rest of

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:29.310
your life.”

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.430
Every day I wake up and every night I go to
sleep thinking that same thing.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.160
Film Courage: Is that your voice?

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.320
Matthew: Yes.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.900
Film Courage: Is that because you’ve had
other jobs?

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.649
Well you’re like “This is what I do.

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:42.220
I want to love what I do even if it gets hard.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.500
And there is difficult people involved.”

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.050
Matthew: Yes, I’ve done other jobs.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.500
I’ve had a lot of jobs.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.219
I’ve done a lot of jobs in film.

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:55.100
I think I’ve pretty much worked every position
on a film set you can.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.149
But there is something about being in love
with your craft that you can never take away

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:01.980
from yourself, that you can never deny.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:07.460
So there is a tremendous amount of uncertainty
being a filmmaker.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:14.960
My mentor (Robert A. Ferretti) he’s made every
film since TANGO AND CASH to DIE HARD to ROCKY.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.950
You know I’ll call him and be terrified
and I’ll say “Bobby, I’m on the edge

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:20.950
of my seat.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:21.960
I don’t know what I’m doing with my life?

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.030
I just want to make movies.”

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.799
And he says “Get with it dude!

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:28.369
I live with the same thing every day.”

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:29.369
Right?

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:30.710
Which is you don’t know when your next movie
is going to come.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.710
You don’t know if you’re going to be wildly
successful.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.740
You don’t know if your movie is going to
be good.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.920
You kind of make each film like it might be
your last one because you’ve got to love

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:46.909
it like that.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:51.700
I don’t I think that in any point of your
career you are faced with that reality and

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.780
those challenges which is you just don’t
do it because it’s the only thing that is

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:57.780
there.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:58.780
It’s the only thing you want to do.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.450
It’s the only thing you can do.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.039
That’s why I make movies.

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:05.829
I think that is what great filmmakers make
movies is because they don’t really have

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:07.450
another choice.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:14.320
Film Courage: So how was that to hear it from
someone that has so many great titles under

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.120
their belt so to speak that they live with
that same anxiety?

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.900
Matthew: It’s refreshing, it’s good.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.450
It makes me understand a little bit more.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.860
It’s kind of the world that has evolved.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.110
It’s like having your own social contract
with yourself.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:37.069
You’ve signed the contract saying “I’m
going to live the life of being a filmmaker”

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:41.220
where you are trying your hardest to make
movies every day and there are no guarantees.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:48.250
Film Courage: Do you think that’s what makes
it so enticing aside from the creative aspect

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:53.840
and seeing this “thing” come together,
the fact that it’s not that guaranteed?

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.220
And then when it does work…

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.450
Matthew: It’s magic?

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.010
Yes, I think that is what’s so enticing
about it.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.460
There’s a lot of moments of magic in making
a film.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.650
There’s the moment of magic when you’re
writing your script and you’ve stared at

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.930
the screen for 10 hours and you can’t figure
out what you want this character to say.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:12.830
You’ve been typing words for hours and hours
and hours and typing in circles and finally

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.590
you crack it with just one line of dialogue
and then you’re off.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.720
And it’s the same moment where you’re
working and working and working and then you’re

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.500
get a little bit of money and then you’re
off making your movie.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.450
And then there’s that moment where you step
on set and you set up your lights and you’re

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.180
blocking the scene with your actors and you
see that scene the way you want to shoot it

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:33.959
and it just flows and you just capture this
perfect scene and you could have never shot

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:36.180
listed it, you could have never planned it,
it just happened.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.769
And that’s cinema, that’s making movies.

